'''Illa Daurada Airport''' ({{lang|ca|Aeroport d'Illa Daurada}}; [[IATA airport code|IATA]]: IDA, [[ICAO airport code|ICAO]]: IDIB) is a regional airport serving [[Illa Daurada]], an island municipality in the [[Badia de Sant Cristòfor]] within the [[Sant Cristòfor (comarca)|Sant Cristòfor comarca]], [[Estret Province]], [[Balboa]]. The airport is the sole air link for the island community of Illa Daurada, a wealthy residential and tourism destination approximately 30 km from central [[Sant Cristòfor]]. It is the operating base of [[AeroTerminus]], the island's only scheduled carrier.

The airport originated as a military airfield constructed by Zong forces during the [[Great Adonian War|Great Adonian War]] Balboa Campaign, and was later converted to civilian use during the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|AC mandate period]]. It is one of the few surviving GAW-era airfields in the Estret corridor still in operation.

== Location ==

The airport is situated on the eastern shore of Illa Daurada, a long, narrow island of 49.7 km² in the [[Badia de Sant Cristòfor]]. The island is one of three major islands in the bay — the others being [[Illa del Portal]] and [[Illa de Matarí]] — and is known for its affluent residential communities and tourism sector. The name "Illa Daurada" derives from the Volisanian for "golden island," a reference to the island's golden sand beaches and its historical association with wealth.

The primary transport link between Illa Daurada and the mainland is the [[Ferries de la Badia de Sant Cristòfor|ferry service]] operated by [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor]], which carries approximately 8,200 passengers daily on the 30.6 km crossing between the Sant Cristòfor main terminal and the island's ferry terminal at Port Daurat. The airport supplements the ferry service, providing a faster but more expensive alternative for residents and visitors.

== History ==

=== Military origins (1936–1947) ===

The airfield was constructed in 1936 by Zong forces during the [[Great Adonian War|Great Adonian War]] Balboa Campaign. Following the initial Volisanian and Balboan invasion of [[Zong Balboa]] in 1935, which seized the [[Balboa Canal|canal zone]] and placed [[Sant Cristòfor]] under siege, Zong forces launched a counter-offensive aimed at pushing towards [[Sant Bart]] in the Jala highlands. The island of Illa Daurada, located within the bay and accessible via Zong naval supply lines, was selected as a forward airbase for bomber operations supporting the counter-offensive.

Zong military engineers laid a 1,400-metre paved runway on the island's eastern shore, along with dispersal pads, fuel storage, and rudimentary barracks. The airfield was used for tactical bombing and reconnaissance missions against Volisanian and Balboan positions during the counter-offensive. The bay's sheltered waters provided protection from naval attack, while the island's proximity to the front lines reduced the distance aircraft needed to fly to reach their targets.

The counter-offensive's progress and the airfield's role in it are documented in Zong military records from the period. After the war ended in 1947, the airfield was decommissioned and the military installations were dismantled, though the runway was left intact.

=== Post-war transition (1947–1977) ===

After the war ended in 1947, the island retained substantial military infrastructure — the runway, dispersal pads, fuel storage, barracks, and road accesses built by Zong engineers. The [[Adonian Community]] coalition forces occupied the island and continued to use the airfield for military logistics and transport until 1952, when the facility was transferred to the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|AC mandate civilian government]].

The mandate administration converted the airfield to civilian use, operating light aircraft for tourist flights to the island's beaches and natural attractions. The runway, at its original 1,400 metres, was adequate for small propeller aircraft but not for jet operations. A modest passenger shelter was constructed from converted military buildings, and the airfield began to appear on civilian aviation charts under the designation IDA/IDIB.

During the 1960s, Illa Daurada became a haven for wealthy Balboans returning to the Estret corridor after the disruptions of the war and mandate transition. The island's isolation, natural beauty, and existing infrastructure made it an attractive location for private residences, and the construction of luxury homes and hotels accelerated through the decade. The airfield's traffic grew steadily as the island's permanent population and tourist visitor numbers increased, though it remained a basic facility serving propeller-driven aircraft and occasional business jets.

=== Civilian upgrade and AeroTerminus era (1977–1988) ===

In 1977, the runway was extended to 1,800 metres and resurfaced to accommodate jet operations, as part of the infrastructure investment accompanying the [[Balboa real-estate bubble|Balboa real-estate bubble]]. The upgrade coincided with the founding of [[AeroTerminus]] by Francesc Riera, a former [[Balboan Defence Forces|naval aviator]] who recognised that the real-estate boom was creating demand for air travel between the island and the canal corridor. AeroTerminus began operations with a {{w|Boeing 737-200|Boeing 737-200}}, offering on-demand charter flights to [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport|Sant Cristòfor]], [[Portnou International Airport|Portnou]], and [[Cecilio Ríos International Airport|Sant Bart]].

The [[Balboa real-estate bubble|real-estate bubble]] burst in 1987, and the subsequent [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis]] eliminated the wealthy clientele on which AeroTerminus depended. The airline went bankrupt in 1988, and the airport's traffic declined sharply. During the crisis years, the airport saw only occasional general aviation traffic and intermittent charter flights.

=== Restructuring and scheduled service (1988–present) ===

Following AeroTerminus's restructuring in 1989, the airline re-emerged as a scheduled regional carrier based at Illa Daurada Airport, operating {{w|BAe 146}} "Whisperjet" services to [[Portnou International Airport|Portnou]] and [[Cecilio Ríos International Airport|Sant Bart]]. The shift from charter to scheduled service transformed the airport's role: rather than serving as a private gateway for the island's wealthy residents, it became an essential transport link for the island community, connecting permanent residents to the mainland for work, education, and services.

The BAe 146 was replaced by the {{w|Embraer E-Jet E2|Embraer E175}} in approximately 2014, {{w|dry lease|dry leased}} from [[Aeroístmus]]. The E175, with 76–88 seats, is better suited to the airport's traffic volume than the larger BAe 146, and its lower operating costs have allowed AeroTerminus to maintain the routes without government subsidy.

== Infrastructure ==

=== Terminal ===

The airport has a single small terminal building, approximately 1,200 m², with two check-in desks, a single security checkpoint, and one departure gate. The terminal has no jet bridges; passengers board aircraft by walking across the apron. A small arrivals hall with a single baggage belt serves both scheduled and charter flights. The terminal includes a small café and a waiting area with views of the apron and the bay.

=== Runway ===

The airport has a single runway, 11/29, 1,800 metres in length, surfaced with asphalt. The runway was originally laid at 1,400 metres by Zong military engineers in 1936 and extended to its current length in 1977 to accommodate jet operations. It is sufficient for regional jet operations up to the size of the Embraer E175 and was previously used by the Boeing 737-200 and BAe 146. The runway's orientation is approximately east-west, aligned with the prevailing trade winds. The approach from the east is over water, with the western approach over the island's residential areas.

=== Access ===

The airport is located on the eastern shore of Illa Daurada, approximately 3 km from the ferry terminal at Port Daurat. A coastal road connects the airport to the island's principal settlements. Travel between the airport and central Sant Cristòfor by air takes approximately 15 minutes, compared with approximately 45 minutes by ferry plus ground transport.

